2026-06-30 15:23:08,892 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-7c9556cf-660f-4dd7-bc5a-7800572b1d61', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\nFor each term, return the best available ontology-resolved value with its accession where indicated.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: organism\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism]\nontology: NCBITaxon (e.g. NCBITaxon:9606)\ndescription: Source species of the proteome of interest in the biological samples analyzed by mass spectrometry. Return the scientific name. \nSource species maybe different than host species when proteins from one organism are expressed in another. \ninstructions: Use NCBITaxon accession. Common examples: Homo sapiens (NCBITaxon:9606),\n  Mus musculus (NCBITaxon:10090), Saccharomyces cerevisiae (NCBITaxon:4932),\n  Escherichia coli (NCBITaxon:562). If multiple species, return a list.\n\nterm: organism part\nsdrf_column: characteristics[organism part]\nontology: UBERON or BTO\ndescription: Main normalized anatomical term for the sample analyzed by mass spectrometry. \ninstructions: Use the clearest ontology-backed anatomy term. Examples: whole-cell lysate, mitochondria,\n  nucleus, cytoplasm, liver, heart, brain, cell, ect. Return null if not applicable\n  (e.g. purified recombinant proteins with no tissue origin).\n\nterm: cell line\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell line]\nontology: CLO, BTO, or EFO (e.g. CLO:0001230 for HEK293)\ndescription: Name of the cell line if a cell line was used.\ninstructions: Return null if no cell line was used (primary cells or tissues).\n\nterm: cell type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[cell type]\nontology: CL, BTO, or CLO\ndescription: Cell type of the sample.\ninstructions: Return null if not applicable or not stated.\n\nterm: material type\nsdrf_column: characteristics[material type]\nontology: PRIDE:0000837\ndescription: Type of biological material analyzed. Differentiates whether the whole cell lysate was analyzed, just a specific anatomical structure or tissue, or if the samples were purified for specific proteins before mass spectrometry analysis. \nallowed_values: tissue, whole cell lysate, organell, membrane, synthetic, purified proteins, affinity captured\ninstructions: Choose the best matching fixed value. Return null if unclear.\n\nterm: strain or breed\nsdrf_column: characteristics[strain or breed]\nontology: free text\ndescription: Strain, breed, cultivar, or ecotype of the organism.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: developmental stage\nsdrf_column: characteristics[developmental stage]\nontology: EFO\ndescription: Developmental stage of the organism or donor.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: technology type\nsdrf_column: technology type\nontology: PRIDE:0000663\ndescription: Type of technology used. For XL-MS this is always fixed.\nallowed_values: proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "proteomic profiling by mass spectrometry" for XL-MS datasets.\n\nterm: source name\nsdrf_column: source name\nontology: PRIDE:0000623\ndescription: Unique identifier for the biological sample. Typically derived from\n  the dataset file names or sample descriptions in the paper.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention used (e.g. "PXD_stem" or "condition_replicate").\n  This is auto-generated from file names; return a description of the sample naming pattern only.\n\nterm: assay name\nsdrf_column: assay name\nontology: NCIT:C178857\ndescription: Unique identifier for each MS run/assay. Typically the raw file stem.\ninstructions: Describe the naming convention. This is auto-generated; return the pattern only.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable (e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"), else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text supporting the extraction, or null\n  "evidence_location": where in the paper (e.g. "Methods", "Abstract"), or null\n\nExample output structure:\n{\n  "characteristics[organism]": {"value": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae", "accession": "NCBITaxon:4932", "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  "characteristics[organism part]": {"value": "mitochondria", "accession": null, "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "...", "evidence_location": "Methods"},\n  ...\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nCross-linking of the Endolysosomal System Reveals Potential Flotillin Structures and Cargo\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nLysosomes are well-established as the main cellular organelles for the degradation of macromolecules and emerging as regulatory centers of metabolism. They are of crucial importance for cellular homeostasis, which is exemplified by a plethora of disorders related to alterations in lysosomal function. In this context, protein complexes play a decisive role, regulating not only metabolic lysosomal processes but also lysosome biogenesis, transport, and interaction with other organelles. Using cross-linking mass spectrometry, we analyzed lysosomes and early endosomes. Based on the identification of 5,376 cross-links, we investigated protein-protein interactions and structures of lysosome- and endosome-related proteins. In particular, we present evidence for a tetrameric assembly of the lysosomal hydrolase PPT1 and a heterodimeric structure of FLOT1/FLOT2 at lysosomes and early endosomes. For FLOT1-/FLOT2-positive early endosomes, we identified >300 proteins presenting putative cargo, and confirmed several substrates for flotillin-dependent endocytosis, including the latrophilin family of adhesion G protein-coupled receptors.\n\n[METHODS]\nLysosomes were enriched from sixty-four 10 cm plates / replicate containing 6 x 106 HEK293 cells per plate using superparamagnetic iron oxide nanoparticles (SPIONs). SPION-containing lysosomes were retained using a magnetic stand in combination with magnetic columns and pelleted. Lysosomal pellets were resuspended to a final protein concentration of 1 mg/ml and cross-linked in two states (intact and disrupted) with the MS cleavable cross-linker disuccinimidyl sulfoxide (DSSO) at a concentration of 0.25 mM. Subsequently, samples were proteolytically digested and fractionated using strong cation exchange chromatography (SCX) into 240 fractions. The 120 fractions containing the majority of cross-linked peptide pairs were analyzed with a MS2/MS3 CID/ETD method on an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
